hadiths[4]{hadithnumber,text}:
"2906","Narrated to us Muhammad ibn 'Isa, narrated to us Salamah who said: Narrated to me Muhammad ibn Ishaq, on the authority of Muhammad ibn Muslim al-Zuhri, on the authority of 'Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, on the authority of al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan ibn al-Hakam, that they both informed him, saying:\n\n\The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) set out in the year of al-Hudaybiyyah"," intending to visit the House"," not desiring to fight. He took with him sacrificial camels"," seventy in number"," and the people were seven hundred men. Each camel was on behalf of ten men.\""\n\nMuhammad said: Then narrated to me al-A'mash"," on the authority of Abu Sufyan"," on the authority of Jabir ibn 'Abdullah al-Ansari who said:\n\n\""We"," the people of al-Hudaybiyyah"," were fourteen hundred.\""\n\n---\n\n**CHAPTER:**"""
"2907","**\nNarrated to us 'Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-'Ala', narrated to us Sufyan. And narrated to us 'Ali ibn Khashram, informed us Ibn 'Uyaynah, from al-Zuhri, from 'Urwah, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah and Marwan, that the Prophet (ﷺ) set out in the year of al-Hudaybiyyah with a little over one hundred men from his Companions. When he reached Dhu al-Hulayfah, he marked the sacrificial animals (qallada al-hady) and made them wear garlands (wa ash'arah), then entered ihram from there.\n\nHe then mentioned the hadith. Abu Bakr said: In the narration of Ibn Ishaq, it is stated: He brought with him seventy camels as sacrificial animals. The people were seven hundred men. He intended that the seventy camels were sacrificed on behalf of the seven hundred men, not that all his Companions who were with him at al-Hudaybiyyah were seven hundred men of the same kind as we say: the term \people\"" may refer to some people"," as Allah"," the Exalted"," says: **\""Those to whom the people said"," 'Indeed"," the people have gathered against you'\""** (Qur'an 3:173). Knowledge encompasses that not all people said this"," nor did all people gather against them. Similarly"," His saying: **\""Then depart from where the people departed\""** (Qur'an 2:199). Knowledge encompasses that not all people departed from 'Arafat"," but He meant by \""the people\"" some people"," not all of them. This is a lengthy topic and not the place for it.\n\nThe narration of Ibn 'Uyaynah explicitly confirms the correctness of this interpretation. Do you not hear him say in the narration: **\""They were a little over one hundred\""**? This makes it clear that all the people of al-Hudaybiyyah were more than one thousand and three hundred"," since \""a little over\"" (bid') refers to between three and ten. This report"," in mentioning their number"," is similar to the report of Abu Sufyan from Jabir that they were fourteen hundred at al-Hudaybiyyah. Thus"," this report also makes it clear that they were one thousand and four hundred. These words indicate that his saying in the narration of Ibn Ishaq"," **\""The people were seven hundred men\""**"," refers to some of the people who were with the Prophet (ﷺ) at al-Hudaybiyyah"," not all of them.\n\nAccording to this interpretation and these evidences"," he sacrificed one camel on behalf of every ten men from some of them"," and one camel or cow on behalf of every seven men from others. Jabir's statement: **\""We shared in a camel sevenfold\""** and **\""in a cow sevenfold\""** refers to some of the people of al-Hudaybiyyah. The report of al-Miswar and Marwan: **\""Ten shared in a camel\""**"," meaning seven hundred of them"," which is half of the people of al-Hudaybiyyah"," not all of them.\n\n---"""
"2908","Narrated to us Al-Husayn ibn Waqid, on the authority of Ilba ibn Ahmar, on the authority of Ikrimah, on the authority of Ibn Abbas (RA), who said:\n\We were with the Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) during a journey. When the time for sacrifice came"," we shared in a cow for seven people and in a camel for ten people.\""\n\n---\nNarrated to us Abu Ammar"," who said: Narrated to us Al-Fadl ibn Musa"," on the authority of Al-Husayn ibn Waqid.\n\n**CHAPTER:**"""
"2909","Narrated to us [someone] and [it was] reported by Rafi' ibn Khadij regarding the division of spoils of war. The Prophet (ﷺ) equated ten sheep with one camel as a guide for the correctness of this issue.\n\n---\n**CHAPTER:**"
